  "summary": "Passive reconnaissance of wikipedia.org (Wikimedia Foundation) shows a mature, well-defended, long-established domain with no malicious reputation signals. One collection source (Wayback Machine) failed; all others returned data.\n\n1. IDENTITY / REGISTRATION\n- Registrar: MarkMonitor Inc. (enterprise brand-protection registrar)\n- Creation date: 2001-01-13 (≈25 years of history)\n- Expiration: 2027-01-13, updated 2025-12-12 / 2026-08-12\n- Registrant: organization redacted for privacy, country US; abuse contact abusecomplaints@markmonitor.com\n- Status: clientTransferProhibited, clientDeleteProhibited, clientUpdateProhibited, active (strong anti-hijack locks)\n- DNSSEC: unsigned (notable gap for a high-profile domain)\n- Name servers: ns0/ns1/ns2.wikimedia.org (self-hosted, in-bailiwick)\n\n2. DNS / E-MAIL POSTURE\n- MX: mx-in1001.wikimedia.org, mx-in2001.wikimedia.org (self-hosted mail, no third-party provider)\n- SPF: present — v=spf1 include:_cidrs.wikimedia.org ~all (softfail, not hard fail)\n- DMARC: present and enforced — p=reject\n- DKIM: no common selectors observed (inconclusive; only standard selectors probed)\n- TXT: Google and Yandex site-verification tokens (expected for a global site)\n- Assessment: e-mail authentication is strong; the only weakness is the SPF ~all softfail, largely mitigated by DMARC p=reject\n\n3. ATTACK SURFACE / SUBDOMAINS\n- Certificate Transparency (crt.sh) returns only 4 names: wikipedia.org, m.wikipedia.org, store.wikipedia.org, zero.wikipedia.org\n- No admin, staging, dev, VPN or backup hosts exposed in CT logs\n- store.wikipedia.org (e-commerce) and zero.wikipedia.org (zero-rated mobile access) are the only non-core entry points\n- Interpretation: very small externally visible footprint relative to the organization's size; most services live on other domains (wikimedia.org, mediawiki.org)\n\n4. HOSTING / NETWORK FOOTPRINT\n- Resolved IP: 185.15.58.224 — Amsterdam, Netherlands, Wikimedia Europe network\n- ASN: AS14907 WIKIMEDIA — Wikimedia Foundation Inc.\n- Announced prefixes: 22 (IPv4 and IPv6, e.g. 185.15.56.0/24–185.15.59.0/24, 208.80.152.0/23, 198.35.26.0/24, 103.102.166.0/24, 2a02:ec80::/48 blocks)\n- Self-owned address space and self-hosted DNS/mail: no dependency on shared hosting\n\n5. REPUTATION / PASSIVE DNS\n- VirusTotal: reputation 88; 60 harmless, 0 malicious, 0 suspicious, 32 undetected; votes 29 harmless vs 1 malicious (single outlier vote, not corroborated)\n- Passive DNS: long history mixing Wikimedia AS14907 addresses with third-party CDN/social ranges (Facebook, Twitter, Dropbox, Akamai-type ranges) — consistent with link-preview/embed resolution and CDN fronting, not with compromise\n- No evidence of historical malicious hosting\n\n6. CODE / ARCHIVE EXPOSURE\n- GitHub: matches are the legitimate wikimedia/mediawiki repository plus many unrelated projects merely citing en.wikipedia.org links in documentation; no leaked credentials, keys or internal configs observed\n- Wayback Machine: query failed (HTTP 503, service unavailable) — historical URL/subdomain data not collected in this run\n\n7. RISK SYNTHESIS\n- Strengths: 25-year registration under brand-protection registrar with all client locks, DMARC p=reject, self-hosted authoritative DNS and mail, own ASN, clean multi-engine reputation, minimal CT-visible surface\n- Weaknesses: DNSSEC unsigned; SPF ends in ~all rather than -all; Wayback history unverified; single uncorroborated malicious VT vote\n- No indication of phishing infrastructure, abandoned/forgotten hosts, or leaked secrets tied to this domain",
  "risk_level": "low",
  "risk_justification": "The domain is a 25-year-old registration held through MarkMonitor with transfer/delete/update locks, self-hosted authoritative DNS and mail under its own AS14907, DMARC enforced at p=reject, a minimal Certificate Transparency footprint (4 names, no admin/staging hosts), and a clean VirusTotal record (0 malicious engines, reputation 88). The only weaknesses are an unsigned DNSSEC zone, an SPF softfail (~all) and one uncorroborated malicious community vote — none of which indicate abuse. Wayback data could not be retrieved, but every other source was consistent and benign, so the residual risk is low rather than undetermined.",
